@meta
id: 08-09
title: Drill — five misspelled keys
chapter: 08
type: drill
skills: change-c
par: 76
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: double-click each key, retype it, five times
open: status.ts

@brief
Five keys in this map are misspelled. Spell them properly:
`pending`, `shipped`, `cancelled`, `refunded`, `failed`.

The rep is three keys and then the word: down, to the front of the line,
change the word, type it, out.

@keys
| `cw` | change to the end of the word |
| `^` | first non-blank character of the line |
| `j` | down a line |
| `Esc` | out of insert mode |

@hints
`j` `^` puts you on the first character of the key, and `cw` replaces it
whole.
--
Do not press `Esc` and then look at the screen. Type the word, `Esc`,
`j`, `^`, and let the next one start before you have checked the last.

@solution
    then five times: j ^ cw<word><Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Everything except the words themselves is five keystrokes a line, and the
words you would have typed in any editor.

What the drill is really building is the habit of getting to the front of
a word before you change it. `^` is a free, exact landing spot, and from
there `cw` has no ambiguity about what it is going to take. Beginners
tend to eyeball the cursor into roughly the right place and then find
`cw` ate half a word. Land, then change.

@start status.ts
export const STATUS_LABELS = {
  pendng: 'Awaiting payment',
  shiped: 'On its way',
  cancelld: 'Cancelled by customer',
  refundd: 'Refunded in full',
  faild: 'Payment failed',
};

@expected status.ts
export const STATUS_LABELS = {
  pending: 'Awaiting payment',
  shipped: 'On its way',
  cancelled: 'Cancelled by customer',
  refunded: 'Refunded in full',
  failed: 'Payment failed',
};
